(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-diagnostic-tests-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Diagnostic Tests Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A test has a negative likelihood ratio of 0.5. What does a negative result mean?","choices":["The patient is half as likely to have the condition","The patient is twice as likely to have the condition","Half of all negative results are wrong","The disease has a prevalence of 50 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, relative to the pre-test probability. A ratio below one lowers the probability, and one above one raises it. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does a sensitivity of 0.5 mean?","choices":["Half the patients testing positive have the disease","Half the healthy patients test negative","The test is half as expensive as the gold standard","An equal chance of identifying a diseased patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A value of one identifies every patient with the disease, and a value of zero identifies none of them. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A test has a positive likelihood ratio of 4. What does that mean?","choices":["Four percent of positive results are correct","A positive result makes it four times more likely","Four patients in every hundred have the disease","The test is correct four times out of five"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, relative to the pre-test probability, which is what makes the ratio a multiplier rather than a probability in its own right. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What characterizes parallel testing?","choices":["Tests are conducted one after another","Only the gold standard is used","The most specific test is done first","All tests are conducted at once"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is used when rapid diagnosis is preferred, at the cost of running tests that serial strategy might have made unnecessary. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Between what values must a probability lie?","choices":["Zero and one hundred","Minus one and one","Zero and one","One and ten"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with zero meaning the event cannot occur and one meaning it certainly will. All the probabilities of events in a sample must sum to one. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why should those conducting each test be blinded to the other's result?","choices":["Blinding increases the prevalence of the disease","One result could influence the reading of the other","Blinding is required for the likelihood ratio to apply","Blinding reduces the cost of the study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is a form of measurement bias: the test would then appear to agree with the standard partly because the reader expected it to. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What limits the use of gold standard tests?","choices":["They are often expensive and invasive","They are insufficiently sensitive","They cannot be compared with other tests","They are affected by disease prevalence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they may not be acceptable to patients, which is why alternative tests are often the initial choice and are then compared against the standard. They may also be hard to complete. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does a likelihood ratio below 0.1 indicate?","choices":["Moderate evidence to rule out","Strong evidence to rule out","Weak evidence to rule out","An indeterminate result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and such a ratio virtually rules out the chance that the patient has the disease, which is the mirror of a ratio above 10 ruling it in. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What does validity mean for a test?","choices":["Getting a similar result on repetition","Its ability to change the post-test probability","Its cost relative to the gold standard","Its ability to determine correctly who has the disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is measured by sensitivity and specificity. Reliability, by contrast, is getting a similar result when the test is repeated under the same conditions. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why does post-test probability matter?","choices":["It determines the sensitivity of the test","It informs treatment decisions","It replaces the need for a gold standard","It is the same as the prevalence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and clinicians need an evidence-based but simple method of arriving at it, which is what the likelihood ratio and its nomogram provide. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"In the classic 2x2 table lettered a to d, which cell is b?","choices":["True positives","False negatives","False positives","True negatives"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a is true positives, b false positives, c false negatives and d true negatives, reading across the top row then the bottom. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"},{"q":"How is the positive likelihood ratio calculated?","choices":["Sensitivity divided by one minus specificity","One minus sensitivity, divided by specificity","Specificity divided by one minus sensitivity","True positives divided by all positives"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is the probability of a positive result in disease over the probability of a positive result without it. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why does it matter whether a test study's prevalence matches your own population?","choices":["Sensitivity will differ if prevalence differs","Predictive values will differ if prevalence differs","The gold standard will classify patients differently","The likelihood ratio will change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and that is the practical consequence of predictive values depending on prevalence: a published value may not transfer to your setting. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Written in the lettered cells, what is sensitivity?","choices":["d over b plus d","a over a plus b","d over c plus d","a over a plus c"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since a is true positives and c false negatives, so the denominator is everyone who actually has the disease. — Assigned reading: The Classic 2 X 2 Table"},{"q":"How is the negative likelihood ratio calculated?","choices":["Sensitivity divided by one minus specificity","One minus sensitivity, divided by specificity","Specificity divided by sensitivity","True negatives divided by all negatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, being the probability of a negative result in disease over the probability of a negative result without it. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does a negative result on a highly sensitive test do?","choices":["It rules disease out","It rules disease in","It establishes the post-test probability exactly","It has no interpretive value"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, remembered as SnOUT. A high sensitivity means few false negatives, so a negative result is unlikely to be missing the disease. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which range of likelihood ratios is indeterminate?","choices":["0.5 to 2","0.2 to 0.5","0.1 to 0.2","5 to 10"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because a result in that band shifts the probability so little that it leaves the clinical question roughly where it was. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is pre-test probability?","choices":["The chance of disease before the test is done","The chance of disease once the result is known","The probability that the test will be positive","The sensitivity of the test being ordered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is often simply the prevalence of the disease in the population the patient comes from. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which tool is used to estimate the pre-test probability of streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["The likelihood ratio nomogram","The receiver operating characteristic curve","The modified Centor score","The 2x2 table"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, scoring temperature above 38 degrees, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical nodes, tonsillar swelling or exudate, and age. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does a likelihood ratio greater than one do to the probability?","choices":["Lowers the post-test probability","Raises the post-test probability","Leaves the probability unchanged","Converts probability into odds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a ratio below one lowers it. A very low ratio, below about 0.1, virtually rules the disease out. It rises above the pre-test value. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What example is given of serial testing?","choices":["Rapid antigen testing alongside throat culture","Endoscopy followed by a hypothetical upper gastrointestinal test","Biopsy repeated after an interval","Exercise tolerance testing, then catheterization if positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, for coronary artery disease: the simpler, safer test runs first and the invasive one follows only on a positive result. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is true of the accuracy of diagnostic tests in general?","choices":["A gold standard test is absolutely accurate","Accuracy has no bearing on interpretation","Accuracy improves with repeated testing","No test is absolutely accurate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, but accuracy still affects the probability of finding disease present or absent, which is why test quality has to be assessed before use. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"When does high sensitivity matter most?","choices":["When a false positive would trigger risky further testing","When the test is expensive","When missing the disease would have serious consequences","When the disease is highly prevalent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because few false negatives is exactly the property needed when the cost of overlooking a case is high. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the mathematical route from pre-test probability to post-test probability?","choices":["Convert to odds, multiply by the ratio, convert back","Multiply the pre-test probability by the ratio directly","Add the ratio to the pre-test probability","Divide the pre-test probability by the specificity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the nomogram exists to spare the arithmetic: a line drawn from the pre-test probability through the ratio meets the post-test probability. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"When is serial testing used?","choices":["When a diagnosis is needed as quickly as possible","When every test is cheap and safe","When only one test exists for the condition","When speed matters less, or tests are risky"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it follows on after simpler and safer tests have proved inconclusive. Costly tests are another reason. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does a likelihood ratio above 10 indicate?","choices":["Moderate evidence to rule in","Strong evidence to rule in","Strong evidence to rule out","An indeterminate result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with 5 to 10 giving moderate evidence to rule in, and the band from 0.5 to 2 being indeterminate. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What defines a false positive in a 2x2 table?","choices":["The new test is negative and the gold standard positive","Both tests are positive","The new test is positive and the gold standard negative","Both tests are negative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and false positives are what a highly specific test produces few of, which is why a positive on such a test rules disease in. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What determines how much parallel testing improves sensitivity?","choices":["How specific each test is","How expensive the tests are","How well each catches what the other misses","The prevalence of the disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: two tests that fail on the same patients add little, whereas complementary tests between them catch far more of the cases. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does a receiver operating characteristic curve plot?","choices":["Positive predictive value against prevalence","Pre-test probability against post-test probability","True positive rate against false positive rate","Sensitivity against disease severity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with one minus specificity on the horizontal axis, which lets clinicians compare alternative tests for the same diagnosis. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which considerations apply when ordering a diagnostic test?","choices":["Risk against benefit, the test's limits, and its cost","Order the most sensitive test available in every case","Repeat every abnormal test at least twice","Order every test the patient requests"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside two instructions: avoid ordering tests indiscriminately, and avoid repeating a test without an indication. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
